<p>Temperatures are continuous-duty maximums in oxidizing atmospheres. Reducing atmospheres lower maximum ratings. See <a href="/conveyor-types/stainless-steel-conveyors/">stainless steel conveyor guide</a> for alloy selection details.</p>
<h2 id="apps-heading">Wire Mesh Belt Applications</h2>
<h3>Oven &amp; Baking Conveyors</h3>
<p>Wire mesh belts are the standard oven conveyor in commercial bakeries, snack food plants, and prepared food facilities. The open mesh allows hot air to circulate around all product surfaces, producing uniform browning and cooking. Baking applications typically use 304 stainless balanced weave or flat wire belts at temperatures from 350-550°F. Ashworth, Cambridge, and Wire Belt Company supply oven belts for commercial baking lines producing bread, cookies, crackers, pizza, and snack foods.</p>
<h3>Freezer &amp; <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/cooling/">Cooling Conveyors</a></h3>
<p>Spiral freezers and IQF (individually quick frozen) tunnels use stainless wire mesh belts to carry products through sub-zero environments down to -40°F or colder. The mesh allows cold air to contact the product underside, accelerating freeze times. Spiral freezers stack a single continuous mesh belt in a helical tower, maximizing freezing capacity in minimal floor space. JBT FoodTech, GEA, and Linde use wire mesh belts in their spiral freezer systems.</p>
<h3>Washdown &amp; Aqueous Cleaning</h3>
<p>Wire mesh belts carry parts through aqueous wash systems, rinse tanks, and drying tunnels. Water drains through the mesh, eliminating pooling. Parts washers in automotive, aerospace, and electronics manufacturing rely on wire mesh belts to move components through multi-stage wash, rinse, and dry cycles. The mesh openings allow spray nozzles positioned below the belt to reach the product underside.</p>
<h3>Heat Treatment &amp; Sintering</h3>
<p>Industrial furnaces use chain-driven mesh belts for continuous heat treatment of metal parts. Applications include hardening, tempering, annealing, brazing, and sintering of powdered metal components. Belt temperatures reach 2,000°F+ in controlled-atmosphere furnaces. Inconel and Nichrome alloy belts withstand these temperatures without excessive scaling or creep. Abbott Furnace, Gasbarre, and Seco/Warwick build continuous mesh-belt furnaces for metalworking applications.</p>
<h3>Coating &amp; Enrobing</h3>
<p>Chocolate enrobing, glazing, and coating lines use fine-mesh wire belts. Product rides on the belt through a curtain of liquid coating. Excess coating drains through the mesh. Air knives or vibration removes additional excess. The mesh pattern should be fine enough that the product does not deform into the openings but open enough for drainage. Food-grade <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">sanitary belt</a> standards apply to enrobing applications.</p>
<h2 id="engineering-heading">Specifying Wire Mesh Belts</h2>
<h3>Mesh Opening Size</h3>
<p>The mesh opening determines the smallest product that can ride on the belt without falling through. For food baking, typical openings range from 1/4" to 1" depending on the product. For parts washing, openings must be smaller than the smallest part. For heat treatment, openings should be large enough for atmosphere circulation but small enough to support the lightest part. Mesh opening is specified as the clear distance between adjacent wires, not wire center-to-center.</p>
<h3>Belt Width &amp; Edge Treatment</h3>
<p>Wire mesh belts range from 6" to over 120" wide. Edge treatment affects belt tracking and product containment. Welded edges seal the outside wire to prevent snagging and product loss. Chain edges use roller chains for positive drive and tracking. Clinched edges fold the outer wire back on itself for a smooth perimeter. The edge type must match the drive method (friction vs. sprocket) and the application's product containment needs.</p>
<h3>Belt Tracking &amp; Support</h3>
<p>Wire mesh belts track on rails, slider bars, or roller supports. At high temperatures, belt elongation from thermal expansion requires spring-loaded or gravity take-up systems to maintain tension. Belt guides or flanged sprockets prevent lateral drift. Proper support spacing prevents belt sag between supports, which causes product shifting and uneven cooking in oven applications. Cambridge recommends support spacing of 24-48 inches depending on belt type and load.</p>
<h3>Belt Life &amp; Replacement</h3>
<p>Wire mesh belt life depends on temperature, load, speed, and belt material. In baking applications at 400-500°F, a quality stainless balanced weave belt lasts 2-5 years with proper maintenance. In heat treatment furnaces above 1,800°F, Inconel belts may last 6-18 months. Belt failure typically manifests as wire fatigue breaks at cross rod connections, edge chain elongation, or mesh distortion from creep at sustained high temperatures.</p>
<h2 id="maint-heading">Cleaning &amp; Maintenance</h2>
<p><strong>Daily cleaning (food applications):</strong> CIP spray bars above and below the belt apply hot water, detergent, and sanitizer while the belt runs at low speed. Bottom sprays are critical because debris lodges on the return side. Effective CIP requires spray pressure of 40-60 PSI and full coverage across the belt width.</p>
<p><strong>Weekly deep cleaning:</strong> Release belt tension, run the belt through an alkaline soak, and brush stubborn deposits from cross rod junctions. Automated belt washers use rotating brushes that scrub both belt surfaces as the belt runs through the washer housing. Cambridge and Ashworth offer belt washer systems designed for their belt profiles.</p>
<p><strong>Lubrication:</strong> Most wire mesh belts in food and high-temperature applications run dry (no lubrication). In ambient industrial applications, food-grade lubricants can extend edge chain life on chain-driven mesh. Never use petroleum-based lubricants on food-contact belts.</p>
<p><strong>Tension monitoring:</strong> Wire mesh belts stretch over time. Excess slack causes tracking problems, sag between supports, and erratic belt speed. Check tension weekly. Spring-loaded take-ups compensate automatically. Screw take-ups require manual adjustment. Replace the belt when it has stretched beyond the take-up adjustment range, typically 2-4% of total belt length.</p>
<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a wire mesh belt conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A wire mesh belt conveyor uses an open-weave metal belt that allows airflow, drainage, and heat transfer through the conveying surface. Essential for ovens, freezers, washdown systems, and drying tunnels. Belt materials range from carbon steel to Inconel for temperatures from -60°F to 2,100°F.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What temperature can a wire mesh belt handle?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Carbon steel: up to 800°F. 304 stainless: up to 1,500°F. 316 stainless: up to 1,600°F. Inconel 601: up to 2,100°F. For freezing, stainless mesh operates down to -60°F. Material choice depends on temperature, atmosphere, and corrosion requirements.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">What are the main wire mesh belt weave types?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Balanced weave (spiral + cross rod, general purpose), compound balanced (denser mesh for small products), and chain-driven mesh (roller chains on edges for heavy loads and high temps). Flat wire and woven wire cloth serve specialty coating and fine-particle applications.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">How do you clean a wire mesh conveyor belt?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>CIP spray bars clean both belt surfaces during operation. Weekly deep cleaning includes releasing tension, alkaline soaking, and brushing cross rod junctions. Automated belt washers with rotating brushes handle daily sanitation in food plants. Bottom sprays are critical for removing debris from the return side.</p>

</div>
